<!--
# Mail, relayed through comail

`mail.bsx` plus `{ComailRelay}` beside the domain, and the observability job
that makes its alarms mean something.

[comail](https://comail.at) is an atproto-identity SMTP relay: you enrol a
domain against a DID you already own, and the relay signs that domain's mail
with a pair of selectors it publishes for you. Nothing in AWS is created for it.
What the deploy declares is four TXT records and five parameters, and what it
VERIFIES is that the enrolment behind them is real.

## Setting it up

**1. Create the atproto account first, and early.** The warming curve is keyed
to the MEMBER's age, not the domain's: days 0 to 7 are capped at 2 messages an
hour and 10 a day, days 7 to 14 at 10 and 50, and only then do the member
defaults of 50 and 500 apply. The clock starts when the account does, so an
account made a fortnight before you need it is a fortnight of warming you do not
have to wait through.

**2. Enrol the domain** at <https://comail.at>, signed in with that account.
This step cannot be automated and is not meant to be: both enrolment endpoints
are gated on an OAuth session cookie whose DID must match the claim, with no
api-key path beside it. Two things to know before you pick a domain:

- **One domain per DID.** Paid tiers exist in the code but ship switched off, so
  the effective cap is one.
- **A subdomain is a separate domain.** Enrolling `example.com` does not cover
  `news.example.com`; every match is exact string equality, in the send API, in
  the SMTP `MAIL FROM` gate and in enrolment itself. A stack relaying two mail
  domains through comail needs two atproto accounts.

**3. Park the five values the response returns**, as SecureString parameters:

    aws ssm put-parameter --type SecureString --region us-west-2 \
      --name /mail-comail-example/dev/comail-did-news-example-com       --value did:plc:...
    aws ssm put-parameter --type SecureString --region us-west-2 \
      --name /mail-comail-example/dev/comail-api-key-news-example-com   --value atmos_...
    aws ssm put-parameter --type SecureString --region us-west-2 \
      --name /mail-comail-example/dev/comail-dkim-selector-news-example-com --value atmos20260904
    aws ssm put-parameter --type SecureString --region us-west-2 \
      --name /mail-comail-example/dev/comail-dkim-rsa-news-example-com  --value 'v=DKIM1; k=rsa; p=...'
    aws ssm put-parameter --type SecureString --region us-west-2 \
      --name /mail-comail-example/dev/comail-dkim-ed-news-example-com   --value 'v=DKIM1; k=ed25519; p=...'

`<ComailEnroll/>` prints exactly these names if any is missing, so you can also
just deploy and read the error.

**4. Deploy.** `<ComailEnroll/>` checks the five parameters exist, checks the key
actually authenticates, hands the selector and its two record values to the
apply as tofu variables, and reports on whether the records resolve yet. The
apply then publishes them, `<StalwartProvision/>` writes one relay route per
enrolled domain, and `<MailProbe/>` sends through it.

## What the records look like, and what is deliberately absent

Two `TXT` selectors off the date-based stem enrolment minted
(`atmos<YYYYMMDD>r._domainkey` for RSA, `...e._domainkey` for Ed25519), an apex
SPF of `v=spf1 include:atmos.email ~all`, and the sovereign `stalwart._domainkey`
selector this stack holds the key for, which every mode publishes.

There is NO `bounce.` subdomain, and that is not an omission. Comail rewrites the
envelope sender per recipient to a VERP address at its own domain, so SPF is
evaluated against `atmos.email` and can never ALIGN with your header From. DMARC
passes on the DKIM signature instead, which is why the probe demands `dkim=pass`
and `dmarc=pass` and does not demand an aligned `spf=pass`.

Keep your own `MX`. Comail's reply forwarding wants the MX pointed at
`smtp.atmos.email`, which would take your inbound mail off this box; replies and
bounce DSNs arrive here directly instead, and `forward_to` stays unset.

## The observability job is not optional trim

Comail pauses a domain automatically at a 5% bounce rate or a 0.08% complaint
rate over a rolling 24 hours with at least ten messages. There is no event
stream on our side to notice that coming: the per-event webhooks exist but their
registration is session-cookie gated, so nothing a deploy runs can subscribe.
What can be automated is the deliverability aggregates endpoint, and
`<ComailDeliverability/>` polls it on a schedule and publishes the numbers into a
`Comail` CloudWatch namespace, where the alarms the domain block emits read them
at 4% and 0.05%, deliberately under the pause.

So the `<ScheduledJobBlock/>` and its `<LambdaJobBlock/>` below are part of what
using comail means. Without them the alarms sit on a metric nothing writes, which
in CloudWatch looks exactly like a healthy one, and your first complaint arrives
as a pause.

## Usage

    beet --main=examples/infra/mail_comail.bsx validate
    beet --main=examples/infra/mail_comail.bsx deploy

`validate` needs only `tofu`. Live deploy needs everything `mail.bsx` needs plus
a completed enrolment. A `429` from the send API during the 14-day warming
window is expected rather than a fault, and the probe logs it and skips the
inbound assertion rather than failing the deploy.
-->
<CliServer {CallOnReady}>
<Router {(RequestLogger, HelpHandler)}>
	<CrateCheck features={["infra", "extra"]}/>
	<Stack app_name="mail-comail-example" region="us-west-2">
		<DeployRoutes/>

		<VpcBlock bx:ref="net" label="net"/>
		<RdsPostgresBlock bx:ref="db" label="db" database="mail" {VpcRef($net)}/>
		<S3BucketBlock label="mail-blobs" deploy_versioned=false force_destroy=false
			runtime_write=true object_versioning=true/>

		<StalwartBlock label="mail" hostname="mail.example.com"
			blob_bucket="mail-blobs"
			{(VpcRef($net), DatabaseRef($db))}
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"mail.example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			ssh_public_key="ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexam you@example.com"/>

		<!-- one enrolled domain, one DID. `{ComailRelay}` takes the hosted relay
		     by default; the relay is AGPL, so a self-hosted one is
		     `{ComailRelay{host:"..", relay_domain:".."}}` and nothing else
		     changes. -->
		<MailDomainBlock
			domain="news.example.com"
			mail_host="mail.example.com"
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"news.example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			alarms_topic="mail-example-alarms"
			{ComailRelay}
			catch_all="replies"
			mailboxes={[
				{localpart:"replies", admin:true},
				{localpart:"probe"},
			]}
			aliases={[
				{localpart:"postmaster", target:"replies"},
				{localpart:"abuse", target:"replies"},
			]}/>

		<!-- the observability pair. The function is invoke-only (no gateway), so
		     nothing but the timer can reach a job that publishes metrics. -->
		<LambdaJobBlock bx:ref="observer" label="mail-observer" exec_route="jobs"
			features="infra,extra"/>
		<ScheduledJobBlock label="comail-deliverability" {InvokeTarget($observer)}
			schedule="rate(30 minutes)" path="comail-deliverability"/>

		<!-- ..and the route it invokes, which is where the poll actually runs. -->
		<Route path="jobs" {HttpServer}>
			<Router>
				<Route path="comail-deliverability" {ComailDeliverability}/>
			</Router>
		</Route>

		<Route path="deploy" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<EnsureSecret secret="db-password" variable="db_password"/>
			<EnsureSecret secret="mail-admin-password"/>
			<EnsureDkimKey/>
			<!-- before the apply, because the selector records read the values it
			     hands over; a variable nobody supplied resolves to empty, and an
			     empty `p=` is a REVOKED selector rather than a missing one. -->
			<ComailEnroll/>
			<TofuApply/>
			<EipReverseDns/>
			<StalwartProvision/>
			<MailProbe mailbox="probe" sender_domain="news.example.com" sender="replies"/>
		</Route>
	</Stack>
</Router>
</CliServer>
